0 Batima, um 1924 - one block, alone in the studio (A3-S02)
0 Name: batima.ldr
0 Author: spex project (hand-authored placement data; not sourced from an official ldraw.org models/ release)
0 // Places `parts/batima-2x4.dat`, which is modelled from PHOTOGRAPHS OF THE
0 // REAL OBJECT - Stefan's own set, with its albums ("BATIMA - Le Jeu de
0 // Construction ideal ... Brevete", French and Dutch, so Belgian; consistent
0 // with Le Batima Soc. and FR 588985, c. 1924). See that file for what the
0 // photographs show and for the four measurements that would replace its
0 // estimated proportions.
0 //
0 // COLOUR 503 Very_Light_Grey, not 15 White. These blocks are CAST STONE,
0 // cream and terracotta, speckled and weathered - not plastic. 503 is dE
0 // 10.1 from the cream in the photographs; 422 Sienna_Brown (dE 7.7) is the
0 // terracotta if the shot ever wants it.
0 //
0 // AND THIS BLOCK IS NOT THE SAME OBJECT AS THE OTHER TWO. Its studs are
0 // hollow rings and its underside is a field of conical sockets: a cone
0 // entering a cone. Kiddicraft's brick and the 1949 one are the same object
0 // as each other, which is what Interlego v Tyco is about - Batima is the
0 // ancestor that already had the idea, in another material and with another
0 // joint. The act is stronger for the difference being visible: 1924 is a
0 // different system, 1940 and 1949 are one object twice.
0 //
0 // REAL SIZE NOW: 38 x 30 x 6 mm, measured on the object. That makes the
0 // Batima module RECTANGULAR - 9.5 mm along, 15.0 mm across - and this is a
0 // 2 x 4, the size that was measured. Height 6 mm = 15 LDU at the piece's
0 // 2.5 LDU/mm, so a block on the ground plane is translated to y = -15.
0 //
0 // It is therefore also much LARGER and much FLATTER than the two bricks
0 // that follow it: 38 x 30 x 6 against a 2 x 2 LEGO brick at 16 x 16 x 11.2.
0 // That is true and the act should show it rather than normalise it - 1924
0 // is a different system, and it does not even share a size.
1 503 20000 -15 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 batima-2x4.dat
